const e=JSON.parse(`[{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","type":"title"},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","anchor":"","type":"content","content":"Reusable GPU vertex animation texture playback for Bevy PBR. The crate targets fixed-topology soft-body VAT first: pre-baked mesh deformation stored in textures, ECS-owned playback state, and a PBR-compatible ExtendedMaterial<StandardMaterial, ...> render path. It is designed for large crowds, ambient motion, one-shot replays, and other cases where moving animation evaluation from CPU-side pose solving to GPU-side texture sampling is worth the memory trade."},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"What Are Vertex Animation Textures?","anchor":"#what-are-vertex-animation-textures","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"What Are Vertex Animation Textures?","anchor":"#what-are-vertex-animation-textures","type":"content","content":"A Vertex Animation Texture (VAT) encodes per-vertex animation data into 2D textures. Instead of evaluating skeletal rigs or physics simulations at runtime on the CPU, the entire animation is pre-baked into image data. At runtime, a vertex shader samples the texture to reconstruct each vertex's position (and optionally normal) at the current frame — entirely on the GPU. Why use VATs? - Zero CPU animation cost — no bone transforms, no skinning, no blend tree evaluation - Excellent GPU batching — all instances sharing the same mesh and material render in one draw call - 10,000+ independently animated characters at interactive frame rates on modern GPUs - Perfect for crowds, vegetation, ambient props, VFX replays, and baked simulations For a complete guide on creating VAT assets, see docs/vat-creation-guide.md."},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Quick Start","anchor":"#quick-start","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Quick Start","anchor":"#quick-start","type":"content","content":"The material must be built once the metadata asset is loaded. See the basic example for the complete asset-loading pattern with buildvatmaterial()."},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Public API","anchor":"#public-api","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Public API","anchor":"#public-api","type":"content","content":"Plugin: - VertexAnimationTexturePlugin - Injectable schedules: activateschedule, deactivateschedule, updateschedule - Default / alwayson(Update) convenience path System sets: - VatSystems::AdvancePlayback - VatSystems::SyncFollowers - VatSystems::ResolveTransitions - VatSystems::EmitMessages - VatSystems::SyncGpuState Consumer-facing components: - VatAnimationSource — links an entity to its VAT metadata asset - VatPlayback — playback state: resolved clip index, startup clip selection, time, speed, loop mode, play/pause - VatCrossfade — request a smooth blend between two clips (named helpers included) - VatPlaybackFollower — mirror a leader entity's playback for modular multi-mesh actors - VatPlaybackTweaks — per-entity options (e.g., disable frame interpolation) - VatAnimationBundle — convenience bundle for VatAnimationSource + VatPlayback Assets and materials: - VatAnimationData — the runtime metadata asset (loaded from JSON) - VatAnimationDataLoader — Bevy asset loader for .vatanim.json and .vat.json files - VatMaterial = ExtendedMaterial<StandardMaterial, VatMaterialExt> — the PBR VAT material - VatMaterialDefaults — resource holding fallback textures - buildvatmaterial(...) — constructs a VatMaterial from metadata, textures, and a base StandardMaterial Messages: - VatClipFinished — emitted when a clip reaches its end (per loop/once/clamp policy) - VatEventReached — emitted when playback crosses a named event frame Validation helpers: - validateanimationdata — metadata-only validation - validatemeshforanimation — proxy mesh validation against metadata - metadataaabb — converts metadata animation bounds into Bevy Aabb - shoulddisablefrustum_culling — central policy helper for culling fallback"},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Playback Features","anchor":"#playback-features","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Loop Modes","anchor":"#loop-modes","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Speed and Direction","anchor":"#speed-and-direction","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Frame Interpolation","anchor":"#frame-interpolation","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Frame Interpolation","anchor":"#frame-interpolation","type":"content","content":"The shader blends between adjacent frames by default. Disable for a \\"stepped\\" look:"},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Crossfade Transitions","anchor":"#crossfade-transitions","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Crossfade Transitions","anchor":"#crossfade-transitions","type":"content","content":"Blend between clips in the vertex shader: The system captures source state, switches to the target clip, blends in the shader, and cleans up when the crossfade completes."},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Animation Events","anchor":"#animation-events","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Animation Events","anchor":"#animation-events","type":"content","content":"Define events at specific frames in the metadata JSON: React to them:"},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Modular Multi-Mesh Sync","anchor":"#modular-multi-mesh-sync","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Modular Multi-Mesh Sync","anchor":"#modular-multi-mesh-sync","type":"content","content":"Keep multiple meshes phase-aligned (body + armor + weapon): Followers mirror clip selection, play/pause, loop mode, and crossfade transitions."},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Metadata Formats","anchor":"#metadata-formats","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Metadata Formats","anchor":"#metadata-formats","type":"content","content":"The crate loads two JSON formats: 1. Canonical (.vatanim.json) — explicit, full control. See docs/asset-format.md. 2. OpenVAT (.vat.json) — compatible with OpenVAT's remap_info.json + layout extensions. See docs/vat-creation-guide.md for complete examples of both formats."},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Dependencies","anchor":"#dependencies","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Dependencies","anchor":"#dependencies","type":"content","content":"- bevy = \\"0.18\\" - serde - serde_json - thiserror"},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Bounds and Frustum Culling","anchor":"#bounds-and-frustum-culling","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Scope","anchor":"#scope","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Scope","anchor":"#scope","type":"content","content":"Implemented in v0.1: - Canonical JSON metadata loading - OpenVAT-compatible metadata normalization subset - Multi-clip playback with configurable loop modes - Crossfade support between clips - Leader/follower playback sync for modular characters - Optional separate or packed normal textures - Shared-material storage-buffer uploads for many independently timed entities - Animation event system (VatClipFinished, VatEventReached) Deferred / documented extension paths: - Rigid-body VAT rotation textures - Auxiliary data textures beyond the metadata model - Bone animation textures - Streaming / clip windowing"},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Examples","anchor":"#examples","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Examples","anchor":"#examples","type":"content","content":"Crate-local lab:"},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Documentation","anchor":"#documentation","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture","slug":"/","heading":"Documentation","anchor":"#documentation","type":"content","content":"- VAT Creation Guide — complete guide to creating VATs from scratch (Blender, Houdini, programmatic) - Troubleshooting — common issues and fixes - Asset Format — JSON metadata schema reference - Configuration — all components, parameters, and tuning options - Architecture — internal design, data flow, system ordering"},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","type":"title"},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Overview","anchor":"#overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Overview","anchor":"#overview","type":"content","content":"saddle-animation-vertex-animation-texture splits VAT playback into four layers: 1. Metadata - VatAnimationData is the canonical runtime description of a bake. - JSON loaders normalize source metadata into this one internal shape. 2. ECS playback - VatPlayback and VatCrossfade are the consumer-facing state. - Systems advance time, resolve loop policy, emit events, and prepare GPU frame selections. 3. Material state - VatMaterialExt owns the data textures, decode uniforms, and a storage buffer handle. - MeshTag is used as the per-entity index into the storage buffer. 4. Shader deformation - The vertex shader samples frame A and frame B, interpolates, optionally crossfades a secondary clip, and feeds the result into Bevy PBR."},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Runtime Data Flow","anchor":"#runtime-data-flow","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Runtime Data Flow","anchor":"#runtime-data-flow","type":"content","content":"1. Load or construct VatAnimationData. 2. Build a VatMaterial with buildvatmaterial(...). 3. Spawn a mesh with: - Mesh3d - MeshMaterial3d<VatMaterial> - VatAnimationSource - VatPlayback 4. VatSystems::AdvancePlayback - resolves VatPlayback.startupclip into a concrete clip index - applies invalidclip_fallback if the resolved clip becomes invalid - advances clip-local time - applies loop policy - advances crossfade source state - records pending events / finish notifications 5. VatSystems::SyncFollowers - copies authoritative playback state from leader meshes into follower meshes - applies optional time offsets after loop-mode normalization - mirrors active crossfade requests for modular multi-mesh actors 6. VatSystems::ResolveTransitions - progresses and clears completed crossfades 7. VatSystems::EmitMessages - emits VatClipFinished - emits VatEventReached 8. VatSystems::SyncGpuState - validates mesh / metadata compatibility - applies metadata-driven bounds and frustum-culling policy - groups entities by material handle - writes one storage-buffer entry per entity - assigns MeshTag so the shader can index the right entry"},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Modular Multi-Mesh Playback","anchor":"#modular-multi-mesh-playback","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Modular Multi-Mesh Playback","anchor":"#modular-multi-mesh-playback","type":"content","content":"VatPlaybackFollower provides a light ECS-level sync layer for modular actors made from several meshes that all share the same VAT metadata layout. - The leader owns the real VatPlayback - Followers skip independent time advancement - The follower sync pass copies the resolved clip selection, play/pause state, optional loop mode, and optional crossfade state - Per-follower timeoffsetseconds is applied after loop normalization so crowds and layered props can intentionally stagger motion without drifting out of phase"},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Material / Shader Path","anchor":"#material-shader-path","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Material / Shader Path","anchor":"#material-shader-path","type":"content","content":"The primary render path is: ExtendedMaterial<StandardMaterial, VatMaterialExt> Bindings: - 100: position texture - 101: position sampler - 102: normal texture - 103: normal sampler - 104: decode/layout uniform - 105: per-entity storage buffer The shader uses mesh UV1 as the baked vertex lookup channel. UV1 is interpreted as a texel-center lookup into the first frame layout. The shader then offsets the row by frameindex rowsperframe."},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Bounds and Culling","anchor":"#bounds-and-culling","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Bounds and Culling","anchor":"#bounds-and-culling","type":"content","content":"Static proxy bounds are usually wrong for VAT motion extremes. The crate addresses this in two ways: - VatBoundsMode::UseMetadataAabb - Inserts an Aabb derived from VatAnimationData::animationbounds - VatBoundsMode::DisableFrustumCulling - Adds NoFrustumCulling World-space playback also disables built-in frustum culling because proxy-local bounds are not reliable there."},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Current Scope","anchor":"#current-scope","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Current Scope","anchor":"#current-scope","type":"content","content":"Shipped now: - fixed-topology soft-body VAT - local-space or world-space metadata flags - separate or packed normal textures - multi-clip playback - shared-material storage-buffer uploads Deferred: - rigid-body VAT rotation / pivot textures - auxiliary shading channels in the shader path - advanced GPU-side instancing extraction beyond the material storage-buffer path"},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Performance Model","anchor":"#performance-model","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Performance Model","anchor":"#performance-model","type":"content","content":"The runtime cost scales as follows: - CPU per-entity: one VatPlaybackRuntime iteration per entity with VatPlayback, plus one storage-buffer write per entity per frame. This is dominated by ECS iteration, not animation math. - CPU per-material: one storage-buffer upload per unique Handle<VatMaterial>. Groups of entities sharing the same material are packed into a single buffer. - GPU per-entity: two texture fetches per frame (frame A + frame B) for position, optionally two more for normals. During crossfade, these double (secondary clip). Each fetch is a nearest-neighbor point sample — no filtering overhead. - GPU per-draw-call: all entities sharing the same material and mesh are drawn in one instanced call. The vertex shader indexes the storage buffer via MeshTag. For crowds of 1,000–10,000 entities sharing one material, the bottleneck is typically vertex shader throughput (texture bandwidth), not CPU-side ECS iteration."},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Message Flow","anchor":"#message-flow","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Architecture","slug":"/docs/architecture/","heading":"Message Flow","anchor":"#message-flow","type":"content","content":"The crate uses Bevy 0.18 Messages (not Events) for VatClipFinished and VatEventReached. Messages are written in VatSystems::EmitMessages and can be read by consumers in any later system. Messages are transient — they only exist for one frame. Clip selection is intentionally split between a high-level startup selector and a resolved runtime index: - VatPlayback.startupclip - metadata default, clip name, or explicit index - VatPlayback.activeclip - the validated runtime index currently driving playback - VatPlayback.invalidclipfallback - policy used when an explicit or stale clip selection no longer resolves cleanly Event detection works by recording \\"traversal segments\\" during time advancement. Each segment represents a contiguous range of clip-local time that was traversed in a single frame. Events fire when a segment crosses a threshold time. This correctly handles: - Normal forward playback - Reverse playback (negative speed) - PingPong direction changes - Multiple loop wraps in a single large delta"},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","type":"title"},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Canonical Runtime Format","anchor":"#canonical-runtime-format","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Canonical Runtime Format","anchor":"#canonical-runtime-format","type":"content","content":"The loader normalizes supported source metadata into VatAnimationData. Required top-level fields in the canonical JSON format: - format - Accepted: vertexanimationtexture@1, vertexanimationtexture - animationmode - Currently supported runtime mode: softbodyfixedtopology - vertexcount - framecount - framespersecond or secondsperframe - decodebounds - clips - positiontexture - coordinatesystem - playbackspace - vertexidattribute - positionencoding Optional top-level fields: - animationbounds - defaultclip - normaltexture - rotationtexture - auxiliarytextures"},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Canonical Example","anchor":"#canonical-example","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"OpenVAT-Compatible Subset","anchor":"#openvat-compatible-subset","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"OpenVAT-Compatible Subset","anchor":"#openvat-compatible-subset","type":"content","content":"Accepted today: - os-remap.Min - os-remap.Max - os-remap.Frames - animations - optional defaultclip / defaultanimation - plus runtime-required layout fields: - vertexcount - texturewidth - textureheight - optional rowsperframe - optional packednormals - optional normalrowoffset This subset is intentionally explicit because raw remap JSON alone is not enough for runtime validation."},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Validation Rules","anchor":"#validation-rules","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Validation Rules","anchor":"#validation-rules","type":"content","content":"Metadata validation rejects: - non-positive FPS - zero frames - zero vertices - empty clip list - default clips that do not match any declared clip name - invalid decode or animation bounds - position textures that cannot address vertexcount texels per frame - position textures whose height and rowsperframe do not cover all baked frames - clip ranges outside framecount - event frames outside clip-local ranges - invalid packed-normal row offsets - unsupported rigid-body mode in v0.1 - unknown loop modes, texture precision strings, normal encodings, normal texture modes, or auxiliary texture semantics in canonical JSON Mesh validation rejects: - missing positions - missing normals - missing UV0 - missing UV1 - proxy mesh vertex count mismatches - malformed UV1 format - UV1 data that does not map to the expected number of unique VAT texels"},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Coordinate and Encoding Assumptions","anchor":"#coordinate-and-encoding-assumptions","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Coordinate and Encoding Assumptions","anchor":"#coordinate-and-encoding-assumptions","type":"content","content":"- UV1 is interpreted as the VAT lookup channel - in DCC terms this is usually “UV2” - absolutenormalizedbounds - sampled values decode directly into absolute source-space positions - offsetnormalizedbounds - sampled values decode into offsets added to the proxy vertex position - coordinatesystem - zuprighthanded is converted to Bevy’s Y-up basis at decode time"},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Precision Profiles","anchor":"#precision-profiles","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Precision Profiles","anchor":"#precision-profiles","type":"content","content":"- exr_half - preferred for production bakes - best precision, higher memory - png16 - acceptable compromise when the toolchain supports it cleanly - png8 - smallest and easiest demo path - highest quantization error"},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Current Non-Goals","anchor":"#current-non-goals","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Asset Format","slug":"/docs/asset-format/","heading":"Current Non-Goals","anchor":"#current-non-goals","type":"content","content":"Documented but not fully implemented in v0.1: - rigid-body rotation textures - auxiliary shading channels in the runtime shader path - dynamic-remesh fluid VAT - streamed clip windowing"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","type":"title"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Plugin","anchor":"#plugin","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Plugin","anchor":"#plugin","type":"content","content":"VertexAnimationTexturePlugin Fields: - activateschedule - Type: Interned<dyn ScheduleLabel> - Effect: turns the runtime on - deactivateschedule - Type: Interned<dyn ScheduleLabel> - Effect: turns the runtime off - updateschedule - Type: Interned<dyn ScheduleLabel> - Effect: hosts all VatSystems Convenience: - VertexAnimationTexturePlugin::default() - VertexAnimationTexturePlugin::alwayson(Update)"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Playback Components","anchor":"#playback-components","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatAnimationSource\`","anchor":"#vatanimationsource","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatAnimationSource\`","anchor":"#vatanimationsource","type":"content","content":"- animation - Handle<VatAnimationData> - Required metadata asset - bounds_mode - VatBoundsMode - Default: UseMetadataAabb"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatPlayback\`","anchor":"#vatplayback","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatPlayback\`","anchor":"#vatplayback","type":"content","content":"- timeseconds - Clip-local playback time - Default: 0.0 - speed - Playback multiplier - Default: 1.0 - activeclip - Resolved clip index into VatAnimationData::clips - Type: Option<usize> - Default: None until metadata or startup selection resolves it - startupclip - Type: VatClipSelection - Default: MetadataDefault - Effect: chooses the first clip to play when activeclip is unresolved - invalidclipfallback - Type: VatInvalidClipFallback - Default: StartupClipThenFirstValid - Effect: recovery policy when the resolved clip index becomes invalid - loop_mode - VatLoopMode - Default: Loop - Note: if left at Loop, clip metadata may provide a default override - playing - Whether time advances - Default: true"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatCrossfade\`","anchor":"#vatcrossfade","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatCrossfade\`","anchor":"#vatcrossfade","type":"content","content":"- fromclip - Source clip index - toclip - Destination clip index - elapsed - Crossfade progress in seconds - duration - Crossfade length in seconds - Clamped to at least 0.0001 Usage: - Prefer VatCrossfade::betweenclipnames(...) or VatCrossfade::toclipname(...) when you have metadata available. - Insert VatCrossfade while leaving VatPlayback.activeclip on the currently playing source clip. - The runtime captures the source clip/time internally and flips playback to toclip. - Do not reset VatPlayback.time_seconds manually when requesting the crossfade."},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatPlaybackTweaks\`","anchor":"#vatplaybacktweaks","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatPlaybackTweaks\`","anchor":"#vatplaybacktweaks","type":"content","content":"- disable_interpolation - false by default - When true, the runtime snaps to frame A instead of blending to frame B"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatPlaybackFollower\`","anchor":"#vatplaybackfollower","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatPlaybackFollower\`","anchor":"#vatplaybackfollower","type":"content","content":"- leader - Entity - Required playback source entity to mirror - timeoffsetseconds - 0.0 by default - Applies a signed clip-local offset after copying the leader state - mirrorloopmode - true by default - When true, the follower copies the leader loop mode before time normalization - mirror_crossfade - true by default - When true, VatCrossfade and its runtime source state are mirrored as well Usage: - Add VatPlaybackFollower to secondary meshes that should stay phase-locked to a leader mesh. - Followers do not advance their own time independently while the component is present. - Offsets are normalized through the resolved loop mode, so looping and ping-pong clips stay stable."},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Enums","anchor":"#enums","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatLoopMode\`","anchor":"#vatloopmode","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatLoopMode\`","anchor":"#vatloopmode","type":"content","content":"- Loop - Wraps at the end of the clip - Once - Clamps at the clip boundary and pauses playback - PingPong - Reflects at both clip boundaries - ClampForever - Clamps at the boundary without toggling playing"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatBoundsMode\`","anchor":"#vatboundsmode","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatBoundsMode\`","anchor":"#vatboundsmode","type":"content","content":"- UseMetadataAabb - Inserts metadata-driven Aabb - KeepProxyAabb - Leaves the proxy mesh bounds untouched - DisableFrustumCulling - Adds NoFrustumCulling"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Material Builder","anchor":"#material-builder","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Material Builder","anchor":"#material-builder","type":"content","content":"buildvatmaterial(...) Inputs: - base StandardMaterial - VatAnimationData - position texture handle - optional normal texture handle - VatMaterialDefaults - mutable Assets<ShaderStorageBuffer> Failure cases: - metadata declares a separate normal texture but no normal handle is supplied"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Messages","anchor":"#messages","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatClipFinished\`","anchor":"#vatclipfinished","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatClipFinished\`","anchor":"#vatclipfinished","type":"content","content":"Emitted each time a clip reaches its boundary (loop wrap, once completion, ping-pong bounce). Fields: - entity — the entity that finished - clipindex — which clip finished - clipname — the clip's metadata name - finishedatseconds — the clip-local time at the boundary Usage:"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatEventReached\`","anchor":"#vateventreached","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatEventReached\`","anchor":"#vateventreached","type":"content","content":"Emitted when playback crosses a named event frame defined in the metadata. Fields: - entity — the entity that crossed the event - clipindex — which clip the event belongs to - clipname — the clip's metadata name - eventname — the event's metadata name - clipframe — the frame index within the clip - normalizedtime — the event's position as a 0–1 fraction of the clip - reachedat_seconds — the clip-local time of the event Events fire exactly once per threshold crossing, even during reverse playback or ping-pong."},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Validation Helpers","anchor":"#validation-helpers","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Validation Helpers","anchor":"#validation-helpers","type":"content","content":"- validateanimationdata - metadata-only validation - rejects undersized position-texture layouts, unsupported loop modes, unknown precision strings, and malformed normal descriptors - validatemeshforanimation - proxy mesh validation against metadata - metadataaabb - converts metadata animation bounds into Bevy Aabb - shoulddisablefrustum_culling - central policy helper for culling fallback"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Utility Functions","anchor":"#utility-functions","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Utility Functions","anchor":"#utility-functions","type":"content","content":"- configurevatdataimage(image) — sets nearest/point sampling on a VAT data texture - makelinearrgba8image(size, data) — creates a linear Rgba8Unorm image with nearest sampling - decodepositionsample(encoded, animation, proxyposition) — decodes a single position sample for debugging - convertcoordinatesystem(value, coordinatesystem) — converts from source coordinate system to Bevy's Y-up - validbounds(min, max) — checks that bounds are finite and min <= max - parsevatanimationdatastr(json) — parses a JSON string into VatAnimationData - parsevatanimationdata_bytes(bytes) — parses JSON bytes into VatAnimationData"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"Clip Selection Types","anchor":"#clip-selection-types","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatClipSelection\`","anchor":"#vatclipselection","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatClipSelection\`","anchor":"#vatclipselection","type":"content","content":"- MetadataDefault - Resolve the startup clip from VatAnimationData::default_clip - Index(usize) - Low-level numeric escape hatch - Name(String) - Resolve the startup clip by metadata clip name"},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatInvalidClipFallback\`","anchor":"#vatinvalidclipfallback","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Configuration","slug":"/docs/configuration/","heading":"\`VatInvalidClipFallback\`","anchor":"#vatinvalidclipfallback","type":"content","content":"- StartupClipThenFirstValid - Re-resolve startup_clip, then fall back to the first clip if needed - FirstValid - Always recover to the first clip in metadata - KeepCurrent - Keep the last validated clip if it still exists"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","type":"title"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Troubleshooting","anchor":"","type":"content","content":"Common issues when working with Vertex Animation Textures and how to fix them."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Visual Issues","anchor":"#visual-issues","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Vertices \\"swim\\" or jitter","anchor":"#vertices-swim-or-jitter","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Vertices \\"swim\\" or jitter","anchor":"#vertices-swim-or-jitter","type":"content","content":"Symptom: vertices wobble or drift slightly from their expected positions. Causes and fixes: 1. Low texture precision: 8-bit PNG textures have only 256 steps per axis. Switch to PNG 16-bit or EXR half-float. 2. Large decode bounds: quantization error scales with the bounding box size. If your decodebounds min/max span a large range, each quantization step covers more world space. Solutions: - Use offsetnormalizedbounds encoding (smaller per-vertex deltas) - Tighten the bounds to the actual animation range - Use higher-precision textures 3. UV1 precision loss**: if the mesh has many vertices, half-precision UVs may round the texel lookup. Ensure your export uses full-precision UVs."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Mesh appears corrupted or exploded","anchor":"#mesh-appears-corrupted-or-exploded","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Mesh appears corrupted or exploded","anchor":"#mesh-appears-corrupted-or-exploded","type":"content","content":"Symptom: vertices fly to random positions, mesh looks like a spiky ball or scattered points. Causes and fixes: 1. Wrong texture filtering: VAT textures must use nearest/point sampling. Bilinear filtering interpolates between adjacent vertices' data, producing garbage. Fix: 2. Mismatched vertex count: the proxy mesh vertex count doesn't match the metadata vertexcount. This often happens because DCC export splits vertices at hard edges or UV seams. Check the export log and update the metadata to match the actual exported vertex count. 3. Wrong decode bounds: if decodebounds min/max don't match the values used during the bake, positions will be decoded incorrectly. Copy the exact values from your DCC tool's output (the remapinfo.json for OpenVAT, or the mesh bounds for Houdini). 4. Coordinate system mismatch: if the texture was baked in Z-up (Blender) but the metadata says yuprighthanded, the Y and Z axes will be swapped. Set coordinatesystem to zuprighthanded for Blender exports. 5. Missing or wrong UV1: the mesh doesn't have a second UV channel, or UV1 was overwritten after the bake. Re-export from the DCC tool."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Normals look wrong (flat shading, dark faces)","anchor":"#normals-look-wrong-flat-shading-dark-faces","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Normals look wrong (flat shading, dark faces)","anchor":"#normals-look-wrong-flat-shading-dark-faces","type":"content","content":"Symptom: the mesh animates correctly but lighting is flat, inverted, or produces dark patches. Causes and fixes: 1. No normal texture loaded: if metadata declares normaltexture: \\"separate\\" but you haven't loaded the normal texture, the shader falls back to proxy normals which won't match the deformed surface. Load and configure the normal texture. 2. Normal texture uses wrong filtering: like position textures, normal textures must use nearest filtering. Apply configurevatdataimage(). 3. sRGB color space: VAT data textures must be loaded as linear (non-sRGB). If the image loader applies sRGB-to-linear conversion, the data will be corrupted. EXR files are inherently linear. For PNG files, ensure they're loaded without sRGB conversion. 4. Packed normals row offset wrong: if using packed normals, verify that normalrowoffset in the metadata matches the actual texture layout."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Animation plays at wrong speed","anchor":"#animation-plays-at-wrong-speed","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Animation plays at wrong speed","anchor":"#animation-plays-at-wrong-speed","type":"content","content":"Symptom: animation is too fast, too slow, or doesn't match the source. Causes and fixes: 1. Wrong framespersecond: the metadata FPS must match the DCC tool's bake FPS. If you baked at 30 FPS but the metadata says 24, the animation will play 20% too slow. 2. Frame count mismatch: verify framecount matches the actual number of baked frames (texture height / rowsperframe). 3. Playback speed**: check VatPlayback::speed. The default is 1.0."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Entity disappears when it should be visible","anchor":"#entity-disappears-when-it-should-be-visible","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Entity disappears when it should be visible","anchor":"#entity-disappears-when-it-should-be-visible","type":"content","content":"Symptom: the entity vanishes when the camera moves, or is only visible from certain angles. Cause: frustum culling uses bounds that don't account for the animated vertex range. Fix: set animation_bounds in the metadata to encompass all vertex positions across all frames (with some padding). Or use VatBoundsMode::DisableFrustumCulling: World-space playback automatically disables frustum culling."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Crossfade produces a visual \\"pop\\"","anchor":"#crossfade-produces-a-visual-pop","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Crossfade produces a visual \\"pop\\"","anchor":"#crossfade-produces-a-visual-pop","type":"content","content":"Symptom: sharp discontinuity at the start or end of a crossfade transition. Causes and fixes: 1. Crossfade duration too short: very short durations (< 0.1s) may not produce visible blending. Increase the duration. 2. Source and target poses very different: VAT crossfade blends vertex positions linearly, which can produce artifacts when the source and target poses are radically different (e.g., standing to lying down). This is an inherent limitation. Strategies: - Add transition clips that bridge between poses - Use longer crossfade durations to smooth the blend"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Loading Errors","anchor":"#loading-errors","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"\\"metadata declares separate normal texture but no handle supplied\\"","anchor":"#metadata-declares-separate-normal-texture-but-no-handle-supplied","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"\\"metadata declares separate normal texture but no handle supplied\\"","anchor":"#metadata-declares-separate-normal-texture-but-no-handle-supplied","type":"content","content":"The metadata JSON declares \\"normaltexture\\": { \\"mode\\": \\"separate\\", ... } but you didn't pass a normal texture handle to buildvatmaterial(). Either: - Load the normal texture and pass it as Some(handle) - Change the metadata to \\"normaltexture\\": { \\"mode\\": \\"none\\" } if you don't need animated normals"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"\\"VAT binding validation failed\\"","anchor":"#vat-binding-validation-failed","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"\\"VAT binding validation failed\\"","anchor":"#vat-binding-validation-failed","type":"content","content":"The system detected a mismatch between the mesh and metadata. Common sub-errors: - \\"proxy mesh vertex count X does not match metadata vertex count Y\\": re-export the mesh or update the metadata. - \\"missing UV1 attribute\\": the mesh doesn't have a second UV channel. Re-export from the DCC tool with UV1 baked. - \\"UV1 format not supported\\": UV1 must be Float32x2. Some exporters use half-precision; check your export settings."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"\\"requested clip index N but metadata only has M clips\\"","anchor":"#requested-clip-index-n-but-metadata-only-has-m-clips","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"\\"requested clip index N but metadata only has M clips\\"","anchor":"#requested-clip-index-n-but-metadata-only-has-m-clips","type":"content","content":"VatPlayback.activeclip is set to an index that doesn't exist. Clip indices are 0-based. With 3 clips, valid indices are 0, 1, 2. Preferred fixes: - Switch to VatPlayback::withclipname(...) or playclipnamed(...) so metadata resolves the clip by name. - Add \\"defaultclip\\": \\"...\\" to metadata and let VatPlayback::default() resolve from metadata. - If numeric indices are unavoidable, set VatPlayback::invalidclipfallback explicitly so recovery behavior is deliberate."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Performance Issues","anchor":"#performance-issues","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Low FPS with many VAT instances","anchor":"#low-fps-with-many-vat-instances","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Low FPS with many VAT instances","anchor":"#low-fps-with-many-vat-instances","type":"content","content":"1. Check draw calls: instances sharing the same material are batched. If each instance has a unique material, you lose batching. Ensure crowd members share a single Handle<VatMaterial>. 2. Storage buffer size: the per-entity storage buffer grows with instance count. This is lightweight (48 bytes per entity) but verify the GPU upload isn't a bottleneck. 3. Texture size: very large VAT textures (>4K) consume GPU memory and bandwidth. Consider reducing frame count, vertex count, or using lower precision."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Compilation / shader warmup stutter","anchor":"#compilation-shader-warmup-stutter","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Compilation / shader warmup stutter","anchor":"#compilation-shader-warmup-stutter","type":"content","content":"The first frame rendering a VAT material may stutter due to shader compilation. This is a general Bevy/wgpu behavior, not specific to VAT. Strategies: - Use shader warmup / pipeline caching (Bevy's built-in mechanisms) - Spawn a tiny offscreen VAT entity early to trigger compilation before it's visible"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"DCC Tool Issues","anchor":"#dcc-tool-issues","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"OpenVAT: exported vertex count differs from Blender","anchor":"#openvat-exported-vertex-count-differs-from-blender","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"OpenVAT: exported vertex count differs from Blender","anchor":"#openvat-exported-vertex-count-differs-from-blender","type":"content","content":"Blender splits vertices at hard edges and UV seams during export. The exported .glb may have more vertices than Blender's edit mode shows. This is normal. Use the post-export vertex count in your metadata."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Houdini: textures look different in engine","anchor":"#houdini-textures-look-different-in-engine","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Houdini: textures look different in engine","anchor":"#houdini-textures-look-different-in-engine","type":"content","content":"Houdini VAT 3.0 can produce HDR (EXR) or non-HDR (PNG) textures. Ensure the texture format in metadata matches what was exported. EXR textures provide better precision but require the engine to support EXR loading."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Animations don't loop cleanly","anchor":"#animations-don-t-loop-cleanly","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Animations don't loop cleanly","anchor":"#animations-don-t-loop-cleanly","type":"content","content":"If there's a visible \\"hitch\\" at the loop point: 1. Frame count: for a clean loop, the last frame should smoothly connect to the first. Some tools bake N+1 frames (the last frame equals the first). In this case, set endframe to N-1 (the last unique frame) so the loop skips the duplicate. 2. Loop mode: ensure the clip uses \\"defaultloopmode\\": \\"loop\\" in metadata, or set VatPlayback::loopmode to VatLoopMode::Loop."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Modular Sync Issues","anchor":"#modular-sync-issues","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Follower doesn't track the leader","anchor":"#follower-doesn-t-track-the-leader","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Follower doesn't track the leader","anchor":"#follower-doesn-t-track-the-leader","type":"content","content":"Symptom: a VatPlaybackFollower entity plays independently instead of mirroring. Causes and fixes: 1. Leader entity ID is wrong: VatPlaybackFollower::new(leader) requires the exact Entity ID of the leader. If the leader was spawned in a different system or frame, ensure you're passing the correct entity. Check with commands.entity(leader).id(). 2. Leader has no VatPlayback: the follower sync system reads the leader's VatPlayback. If the leader is missing this component, the follower will be skipped silently. 3. Leader despawned: if the leader entity was despawned, the follower can't find it. The follower will continue playing independently in this case."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Follower crossfade out of sync","anchor":"#follower-crossfade-out-of-sync","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Follower crossfade out of sync","anchor":"#follower-crossfade-out-of-sync","type":"content","content":"Symptom: leader and follower crossfade at different speeds or to different clips. Fix: ensure mirror_crossfade is true (default). The follower sync system copies the exact crossfade state from the leader, including elapsed time and duration."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Programmatic VAT Issues","anchor":"#programmatic-vat-issues","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Texture data looks correct but mesh doesn't animate","anchor":"#texture-data-looks-correct-but-mesh-doesn-t-animate","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Texture data looks correct but mesh doesn't animate","anchor":"#texture-data-looks-correct-but-mesh-doesn-t-animate","type":"content","content":"Symptom: you built the VAT texture in code but the mesh stays in its rest pose. Causes and fixes: 1. Missing MeshMaterial3d<VatMaterial>: the entity must use the VAT material, not a standard StandardMaterial. Without the VAT material, the vertex shader never samples the animation texture. 2. Storage buffer not populated: buildvatmaterial() creates an initial single-entry storage buffer. The syncgpustate system updates it each frame. Ensure the plugin is added and the entity has both VatAnimationSource and VatPlayback. 3. Texture not configured for nearest sampling: call configurevatdataimage() on your generated Image or use makelinearrgba8image() which does this automatically. 4. UV1 coordinates off by half a texel: UV1 values must point to texel centers, not edges. The formula is (texelindex + 0.5) / texturedimension. An off-by-one here causes each vertex to read its neighbor's animation data."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Position data encodes correctly but decodes wrong","anchor":"#position-data-encodes-correctly-but-decodes-wrong","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Position data encodes correctly but decodes wrong","anchor":"#position-data-encodes-correctly-but-decodes-wrong","type":"content","content":"Symptom: decodepositionsample() returns wrong values for hand-crafted texture data. Causes and fixes: 1. 8-bit quantization: Rgba8Unorm stores values as u8 / 255.0. The round-trip encode → store → decode loses precision. For values close to 0 or 1, the quantization error can shift vertices noticeably. Use higher precision formats for production. 2. Bounds mismatch: the decodeboundsmin and decodeboundsmax in the metadata must exactly match what was used to encode the texture data. Even a small mismatch scales all positions incorrectly."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Build and Integration Issues","anchor":"#build-and-integration-issues","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"\\"No method named \`add_message\` found\\"","anchor":"#no-method-named-add-message-found","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"\\"No method named \`add_message\` found\\"","anchor":"#no-method-named-add-message-found","type":"content","content":"You're on an older Bevy version. This crate requires Bevy 0.18 which introduced the Message trait and add_message(). Upgrade your Bevy dependency."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Shader compilation errors on first frame","anchor":"#shader-compilation-errors-on-first-frame","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Shader compilation errors on first frame","anchor":"#shader-compilation-errors-on-first-frame","type":"content","content":"The ExtendedMaterial<StandardMaterial, VatMaterialExt> shader compiles on first use, which can cause a frame stutter. This is standard Bevy/wgpu behavior. To mitigate: - Spawn a tiny offscreen VAT entity during loading to trigger compilation early - Use Bevy's pipeline caching if available"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Multiple VAT materials cause separate draw calls","anchor":"#multiple-vat-materials-cause-separate-draw-calls","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Multiple VAT materials cause separate draw calls","anchor":"#multiple-vat-materials-cause-separate-draw-calls","type":"content","content":"Each unique Handle<VatMaterial> produces a separate draw call and storage buffer. For crowds, all instances should share one material handle:"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Validation Utilities","anchor":"#validation-utilities","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"Troubleshooting","slug":"/docs/troubleshooting/","heading":"Validation Utilities","anchor":"#validation-utilities","type":"content","content":"The crate provides validation helpers you can call manually for debugging: Use decodepositionsample() to spot-check that a specific encoded sample decodes to the expected world-space position from your DCC tool."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","type":"title"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"VAT Creation Guide","anchor":"","type":"content","content":"A complete guide to creating, exporting, and using Vertex Animation Textures with this crate."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"What Are Vertex Animation Textures?","anchor":"#what-are-vertex-animation-textures","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"What Are Vertex Animation Textures?","anchor":"#what-are-vertex-animation-textures","type":"content","content":"A Vertex Animation Texture (VAT) encodes per-vertex animation data into 2D textures. Instead of evaluating skeletal rigs or physics simulations at runtime on the CPU, the entire animation is pre-baked into image data. At runtime, a vertex shader samples the texture to reconstruct each vertex's position (and optionally normal) at the current frame — entirely on the GPU. Texture layout: the X axis maps to individual vertices, and the Y axis maps to animation frames (time). Each pixel stores the XYZ position of one vertex at one frame, encoded into the RGB channels. A second UV channel on the mesh (UV1 in engine terms) encodes each vertex's lookup coordinate into the texture."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"When to Use VATs (vs Alternatives)","anchor":"#when-to-use-vats-vs-alternatives","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"When to Use VATs (vs Alternatives)","anchor":"#when-to-use-vats-vs-alternatives","type":"content","content":"Use VATs when: - You need hundreds or thousands of independently animated instances (crowds, vegetation, ambient props, particle-like effects) - The animation is fully pre-determined — no runtime IK, ragdoll, or procedural adjustment needed - You want to offload animation entirely to the GPU for maximum draw-call batching - You're baking simulation results (cloth, fluid, destruction) that are too expensive to run at runtime Avoid VATs when: - You need runtime animation blending beyond simple crossfades between pre-baked clips - You need inverse kinematics, ragdoll physics, or other runtime pose adjustments - The animation has very few instances (skeletal is simpler for 1–10 characters) - Texture memory is extremely constrained (mobile with many unique animations)"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"VAT Types","anchor":"#vat-types","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Soft Body (Fixed Topology) — supported","anchor":"#soft-body-fixed-topology-supported","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Soft Body (Fixed Topology) — supported","anchor":"#soft-body-fixed-topology-supported","type":"content","content":"The mesh deforms but keeps the same vertex count and order every frame. This is the most common type and is what this crate fully implements. Use cases: cloth, flags, waving vegetation, character animations, organic deformation, any simulation where topology doesn't change. Output: position texture + optional normal texture + proxy mesh."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Rigid Body — deferred (documented extension path)","anchor":"#rigid-body-deferred-documented-extension-path","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Rigid Body — deferred (documented extension path)","anchor":"#rigid-body-deferred-documented-extension-path","type":"content","content":"Fracture and destruction simulations where each piece is a rigid body. Instead of per-vertex positions, the texture stores per-piece transforms (translation + quaternion rotation). Each mesh piece has a pivot point encoded in vertex colors or UV3. Use cases: glass breaking, concrete crumbling, explosions, RBD simulations."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Fluid / Dynamic Remesh — deferred","anchor":"#fluid-dynamic-remesh-deferred","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Fluid / Dynamic Remesh — deferred","anchor":"#fluid-dynamic-remesh-deferred","type":"content","content":"Simulations where topology changes every frame (different vertex counts and connectivity). Requires a Lookup Table Texture (LUT) to map triangles to animation coordinates. Use cases: fluid simulations, smoke, morphing effects."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Sprite / Particle — deferred","anchor":"#sprite-particle-deferred","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Sprite / Particle — deferred","anchor":"#sprite-particle-deferred","type":"content","content":"Each particle is a small card (quad). The position texture stores per-particle positions over time. Cards are billboarded in the shader. Use cases: sparks, debris, rain, magic effects, particle simulations."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step-by-Step: Creating a VAT in Blender (OpenVAT)","anchor":"#step-by-step-creating-a-vat-in-blender-openvat","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Prerequisites","anchor":"#prerequisites","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Prerequisites","anchor":"#prerequisites","type":"content","content":"- Blender 4.2 LTS or newer - OpenVAT addon installed"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 1: Prepare Your Animation","anchor":"#step-1-prepare-your-animation","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 1: Prepare Your Animation","anchor":"#step-1-prepare-your-animation","type":"content","content":"Animate your mesh using any method: armature, shape keys, Geometry Nodes, physics simulations, or modifiers. The only requirement is that the vertex count and vertex order remain constant across all frames (fixed topology). > Important: if your mesh has hard edges or UV seams, Blender may split vertices during > export. This changes the effective vertex count. OpenVAT handles this automatically, but > be aware that the exported vertex count may differ from what you see in edit mode."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 2: Select the Target Mesh","anchor":"#step-2-select-the-target-mesh","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 2: Select the Target Mesh","anchor":"#step-2-select-the-target-mesh","type":"content","content":"Select the mesh object (or collection of meshes) you want to bake."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 3: Open the OpenVAT Panel","anchor":"#step-3-open-the-openvat-panel","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 3: Open the OpenVAT Panel","anchor":"#step-3-open-the-openvat-panel","type":"content","content":"In the 3D Viewport sidebar (press N), find the OpenVAT panel."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 4: Configure Encoding Settings","anchor":"#step-4-configure-encoding-settings","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 5: Define Animation Clips (OpenVAT 1.1.0+)","anchor":"#step-5-define-animation-clips-openvat-1-1-0","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 5: Define Animation Clips (OpenVAT 1.1.0+)","anchor":"#step-5-define-animation-clips-openvat-1-1-0","type":"content","content":"In the Animation Data panel, define named clips with their frame ranges and loop flags: - idle: frames 0–23, looping - walk: frames 24–47, looping - attack: frames 48–71, play once These are metadata only — they don't change which frames are baked, but they're written to the output JSON for runtime clip selection."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 6: Set Output Directory and Encode","anchor":"#step-6-set-output-directory-and-encode","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 6: Set Output Directory and Encode","anchor":"#step-6-set-output-directory-and-encode","type":"content","content":"Click Encode. OpenVAT will: 1. Capture every frame's vertex positions 2. Compute the bounding box across all frames (the \\"remap bounds\\") 3. Normalize positions to [0, 1] range using the bounds 4. Write the position texture 5. Optionally write the normal texture 6. Export the proxy mesh with UV1 baked 7. Write remap_info.json with decode metadata"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 7: Verify Output","anchor":"#step-7-verify-output","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 7: Verify Output","anchor":"#step-7-verify-output","type":"content","content":"Your output directory should contain:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 8: Preview in Blender","anchor":"#step-8-preview-in-blender","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 8: Preview in Blender","anchor":"#step-8-preview-in-blender","type":"content","content":"After encoding, OpenVAT creates a copy of the mesh with a decoder modifier. Play the timeline to verify the animation reconstructs correctly in Blender's viewport."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step-by-Step: Creating a VAT in Houdini","anchor":"#step-by-step-creating-a-vat-in-houdini","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step-by-Step: Creating a VAT in Houdini","anchor":"#step-by-step-creating-a-vat-in-houdini","type":"content","content":"SideFX Labs VAT 3.0 is the industry-standard tool for VAT creation."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Workflow","anchor":"#workflow","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Workflow","anchor":"#workflow","type":"content","content":"1. Feed your simulation geometry (Vellum cloth, RBD, FLIP fluid, particles) into the Labs Vertex Animation Textures 3.0 ROP node. 2. Choose the animation type: Soft, Rigid, Fluid, or Sprite. 3. Configure texture format (HDR EXR or non-HDR PNG), dimensions, and precision. 4. The tool outputs: FBX mesh, position texture, optional rotation/normal/color textures, and bounding box data embedded in the mesh. Houdini VAT 3.0 also supports LODs, up to 9 custom data channels, conditional triggering, and power-of-two texture padding."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Importing into Bevy with This Crate","anchor":"#importing-into-bevy-with-this-crate","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Asset Setup","anchor":"#asset-setup","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Asset Setup","anchor":"#asset-setup","type":"content","content":"After exporting from your DCC tool, you need: 1. Proxy mesh (.glb or .gltf) — the reference mesh with UV1 baked 2. Position texture (.exr or .png) — the baked position data 3. Normal texture (.exr or .png, optional) — the baked normal data 4. Metadata JSON (.vatanim.json or .vat.json) — decode parameters Place these in your Bevy assets/ directory."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Writing the Metadata JSON","anchor":"#writing-the-metadata-json","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Canonical Format (recommended)","anchor":"#canonical-format-recommended","type":"heading","level":4},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Canonical Format (recommended)","anchor":"#canonical-format-recommended","type":"content","content":"The canonical format is the most explicit and gives you full control:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"OpenVAT Format (if using OpenVAT export)","anchor":"#openvat-format-if-using-openvat-export","type":"heading","level":4},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"OpenVAT Format (if using OpenVAT export)","anchor":"#openvat-format-if-using-openvat-export","type":"content","content":"OpenVAT's remap_info.json is also supported. You may need to add layout fields that OpenVAT doesn't include by default:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Loading and Spawning in Bevy","anchor":"#loading-and-spawning-in-bevy","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Configuring Textures","anchor":"#configuring-textures","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Configuring Textures","anchor":"#configuring-textures","type":"content","content":"VAT textures must use nearest/point filtering and should not have mipmaps:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Texture Encoding Deep Dive","anchor":"#texture-encoding-deep-dive","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Position Encoding","anchor":"#position-encoding","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Position Encoding","anchor":"#position-encoding","type":"content","content":"At bake time, positions are normalized into [0, 1]: At runtime, the shader reverses this: The bounding box min/max values are the decode_bounds in the metadata JSON."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Two Position Encoding Modes","anchor":"#two-position-encoding-modes","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Two Position Encoding Modes","anchor":"#two-position-encoding-modes","type":"content","content":"- absolutenormalizedbounds: the decoded value is the absolute vertex position in source space. The proxy mesh position is replaced entirely. - offsetnormalizedbounds: the decoded value is an offset (delta) from the proxy mesh vertex. The proxy mesh position is added to the decoded offset. Most Houdini exports use absolute encoding. OpenVAT uses offset encoding by default."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Normal Encoding","anchor":"#normal-encoding","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Normal Encoding","anchor":"#normal-encoding","type":"content","content":"Unit normals (range [-1, 1]) are remapped to [0, 1]: Decoded in shader:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Normal Storage Options","anchor":"#normal-storage-options","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Precision Profiles","anchor":"#precision-profiles","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Precision Profiles","anchor":"#precision-profiles","type":"content","content":"The precision trade-off: quantization error scales with the bounding box size. A large bounding box means each quantization step covers more world-space distance. Strategies: - Use offset encoding (smaller per-vertex deltas = smaller bounding box) - Use higher-precision textures (EXR half or PNG 16) - Keep animations spatially compact"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Coordinate Systems","anchor":"#coordinate-systems","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Coordinate Systems","anchor":"#coordinate-systems","type":"content","content":"This crate handles coordinate conversion automatically via the coordinatesystem metadata field. Set it to zuprighthanded for Blender exports, and the shader will convert (x, y, z) to Bevy's Y-up space: (x, z, -y)."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"UV1 — The Vertex Lookup Channel","anchor":"#uv1-the-vertex-lookup-channel","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"UV1 — The Vertex Lookup Channel","anchor":"#uv1-the-vertex-lookup-channel","type":"content","content":"The mesh must have a second UV channel (UV1 in Bevy, \\"UV2\\" in most DCC tools). This channel maps each vertex to its corresponding texel in the VAT texture. Rules: - Each vertex must map to a unique texel. Shared UV1 values cause vertices to read the same animation data. - UV1 values encode texel-center coordinates: uv = (texelindex + 0.5) / texturewidth - The DCC export tool (OpenVAT, Houdini VAT) generates UV1 automatically during the bake. - Do not manually edit UV1 after baking — even small rounding errors cause vertex swimming. Verifying UV1: the crate's validatemeshfor_animation() function checks that UV1 exists, has the correct format, and maps to the expected number of unique texels."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Multi-Clip Workflow","anchor":"#multi-clip-workflow","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Multi-Clip Workflow","anchor":"#multi-clip-workflow","type":"content","content":"A single VAT bake can contain multiple animation clips, each using a sub-range of the texture's frame rows: Define clips in the metadata JSON: Switch clips at runtime:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Playback Configuration","anchor":"#playback-configuration","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Loop Modes","anchor":"#loop-modes","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Speed Control","anchor":"#speed-control","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Frame Interpolation","anchor":"#frame-interpolation","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Frame Interpolation","anchor":"#frame-interpolation","type":"content","content":"By default, the shader blends between adjacent frames for smooth playback. Disable this for a \\"stepped\\" or \\"sprite-sheet\\" look:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Crossfade Transitions","anchor":"#crossfade-transitions","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Crossfade Transitions","anchor":"#crossfade-transitions","type":"content","content":"Crossfade blends two clips simultaneously during a transition:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Animation Events","anchor":"#animation-events","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Animation Events","anchor":"#animation-events","type":"content","content":"Define events at specific frames in the metadata: React to them with Bevy observers:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Modular Multi-Mesh Actors","anchor":"#modular-multi-mesh-actors","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Modular Multi-Mesh Actors","anchor":"#modular-multi-mesh-actors","type":"content","content":"For characters assembled from multiple meshes (body + armor + weapon), use the leader/follower system to keep all parts synchronized: Followers automatically mirror clip selection, play/pause state, loop mode, and crossfade transitions from the leader."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Bounds and Frustum Culling","anchor":"#bounds-and-frustum-culling","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Bounds and Frustum Culling","anchor":"#bounds-and-frustum-culling","type":"content","content":"Static proxy mesh bounds are almost always wrong for VAT because vertices move far beyond the rest pose. Three strategies: Set the bounds mode per-entity: > Tip: define animationbounds in your metadata JSON as a slightly-padded bounding box > that encompasses all vertex positions across all frames. This is separate from > decode_bounds (which defines the texture encoding range)."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Performance Characteristics","anchor":"#performance-characteristics","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Why VATs Excel at Crowds","anchor":"#why-vats-excel-at-crowds","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Why VATs Excel at Crowds","anchor":"#why-vats-excel-at-crowds","type":"content","content":"- Zero CPU animation cost: no bone transforms, no skinning, no blend tree evaluation - Excellent GPU batching: all instances sharing the same mesh and material can be drawn together. This crate uses a single storage buffer per material for all instances. - Minimal per-instance data: only frame indices and blend weights (12 floats per entity in the storage buffer)"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Benchmark Reference Points","anchor":"#benchmark-reference-points","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Benchmark Reference Points","anchor":"#benchmark-reference-points","type":"content","content":"- 10,000 independently animated characters at 30+ FPS is achievable with modern GPUs - A single draw call can render all instances sharing the same VAT material - CPU-side cost scales with entity count (ECS iteration), not vertex count"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Memory Budget","anchor":"#memory-budget","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Memory Budget","anchor":"#memory-budget","type":"content","content":"Position texture memory: width height bytesperpixel Normal textures double these numbers. For crowds, all instances share the same textures."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Full Worked Example: Animated Flag","anchor":"#full-worked-example-animated-flag","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"1. In Blender","anchor":"#1-in-blender","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"1. In Blender","anchor":"#1-in-blender","type":"content","content":"1. Create a subdivided plane (10x10 segments = 121 vertices). 2. Add a Cloth simulation with some wind. 3. Bake the simulation (frames 1–48). 4. Install and open OpenVAT. 5. Set frame range 1–48, normal encoding: Separate, format: PNG 16, mesh: glTF. 6. Define one clip: \\"wave\\", frames 0–47, looping. 7. Click Encode."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"2. File Organization","anchor":"#2-file-organization","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"3. Metadata JSON","anchor":"#3-metadata-json","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"4. Bevy Code","anchor":"#4-bevy-code","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Programmatic VAT Generation (No DCC Tool Required)","anchor":"#programmatic-vat-generation-no-dcc-tool-required","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Programmatic VAT Generation (No DCC Tool Required)","anchor":"#programmatic-vat-generation-no-dcc-tool-required","type":"content","content":"You can create VAT data entirely in Rust at startup or build time — useful for procedural animations, unit testing, or prototyping without any Blender/Houdini dependency."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Overview","anchor":"#overview","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Overview","anchor":"#overview","type":"content","content":"To create a VAT programmatically you need three things: 1. A proxy mesh with UV1 baked (mapping each vertex to its texel in the VAT texture) 2. A position texture (and optionally a normal texture) containing per-vertex, per-frame data 3. A VatAnimationData struct describing the bake layout"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 1: Generate the Proxy Mesh","anchor":"#step-1-generate-the-proxy-mesh","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 1: Generate the Proxy Mesh","anchor":"#step-1-generate-the-proxy-mesh","type":"content","content":"Build a Mesh with ATTRIBUTEPOSITION, ATTRIBUTENORMAL, ATTRIBUTEUV0, and ATTRIBUTEUV1. UV1 maps each vertex to a unique texel:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 2: Generate the Position Texture","anchor":"#step-2-generate-the-position-texture","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 2: Generate the Position Texture","anchor":"#step-2-generate-the-position-texture","type":"content","content":"For each frame, compute each vertex's position and encode it into [0, 1]:"},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 3: Build VatAnimationData","anchor":"#step-3-build-vatanimationdata","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 4: Assemble and Spawn","anchor":"#step-4-assemble-and-spawn","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Step 4: Assemble and Spawn","anchor":"#step-4-assemble-and-spawn","type":"content","content":"The crate's own examples use this exact approach — see examples/support/src/lib.rs for a complete working implementation that generates a waving mesh entirely in code."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Advanced Optimization Techniques","anchor":"#advanced-optimization-techniques","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Reducing Texture Memory","anchor":"#reducing-texture-memory","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Reducing Texture Memory","anchor":"#reducing-texture-memory","type":"content","content":"1. Offset encoding: use offsetnormalizedbounds to store per-vertex deltas from the proxy pose. Deltas are typically much smaller than absolute positions, so the decode bounds shrink and quantization error decreases — even 8-bit PNG can work well for small-deformation animations like cloth or facial blendshapes. 2. Reduce frame count: export every other frame and let the shader interpolate. The crate interpolates between adjacent frames by default, so halving the frame count halves texture height with barely visible quality loss. 3. Reduce vertex count: use a lower-resolution simulation mesh for the VAT bake. The rendering mesh can have more visual detail in UV0 (normal maps, textures) while the VAT drives a simplified vertex set. 4. Pack normals: use packed normals in the position texture instead of a separate normal texture. This halves the number of textures but doubles rowsperframe."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"LOD Strategies","anchor":"#lod-strategies","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"LOD Strategies","anchor":"#lod-strategies","type":"content","content":"Since all VAT instances share the same texture data, traditional mesh LOD (swapping meshes) requires separate VAT bakes per LOD level. Strategies: 1. Distance-based interpolation toggle: disable frame interpolation (VatPlaybackTweaks::disableinterpolation) for distant instances to halve texture fetches. 2. Speed reduction: slow down or pause playback for off-screen or very distant instances — no GPU cost when playing is false. 3. Separate VAT bakes**: create lower-vertex-count bakes for distant LODs, each with their own metadata and textures. Switch using Bevy's standard LOD or visibility systems."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Crowd Rendering Best Practices","anchor":"#crowd-rendering-best-practices","type":"heading","level":3},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Crowd Rendering Best Practices","anchor":"#crowd-rendering-best-practices","type":"content","content":"1. Share materials: all instances using the same VAT should share a single Handle<VatMaterial>. The crate batches all entities per material into one storage buffer upload and one draw call. 2. Stagger phase offsets: give each instance a different VatPlayback::timeseconds start value to avoid the \\"synchronized marching\\" look. A hash of the entity position works well. 3. Mix clips: randomly assign different clips (idle, walk variants) to break visual repetition. The metadata supports multiple clips in a single texture. 4. Use animation events**: attach footstep sounds or dust particle spawns to VatEventReached messages instead of polling playback time."},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Glossary","anchor":"#glossary","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Further Reading","anchor":"#further-reading","type":"heading","level":2},{"title":"VAT Creation Guide","slug":"/docs/vat-creation-guide/","heading":"Further Reading","anchor":"#further-reading","type":"content","content":"- SideFX Labs VAT 3.0 Documentation - OpenVAT Blender Extension - OpenVAT Website - NVIDIA GPU Gems 3 — Animated Crowd Rendering - Stoyan Dimitrov — VAT Technical Breakdown - Snap Lens Studio — VAT Guide (covers all 4 VAT types) - Wildlife Studios — Texture Animation Techniques - Unity Mesh-Animation — GPU VAT Library - Godot VAT Plugin"}]`);export{e as default};
